a Texas man facing the death penalty  found an unlikely supporter fighting to  save his life one of the people he  wanted killed Bart Whitaker was  convicted of hiring a hitman to target  his own family his mother and brother  were murdered and his father was  seriously hurt a jury found Whitaker  guilty in 2007 of arranging the plot and  he was sentenced to death  his father Kent forgave his son and  fought for years to get Bart off death  row  Peter Van Sant gives us an update in  this week's 48 hours the Whitaker family  just seemed like the model typical  well-to-do Sugarland family Kent would  occur his wife Tricia and there are two  grown sons Bart and Kevin were returning  home after dinner on December 10th 2003  when that picture-perfect image was  shattered kevin opens the door there's a  huge loud noise bang kevin was shot  center mass right in his sternum Tricia  says oh no and I hear another noise bang  Tricia had made it into the house behind  Kevin and was shot and then fell in the  doorway but I look inside and there is a  figure with a ski mask on Bart didn't  chase the guy into the house I'm as he  approached that figure he was shot I  think oh my god he shot all four of us  well Bart and his father can't recovered  from their wounds Tricia and Kevin died  it became clear that it was a conspiracy  it was not a random act of violence  detective slot soon discovered the man  behind the conspiracy was none other  than Bart Whitaker a son who hated his  family so much he hired a hitman to kill  them all Bart had the hit man he hired  shoot him to cover up his crime in 2007  Bart was tried convicted and given the  death penalty I believe as a Christian  that God can and does forgive and change  people's hearts over the next 10 years  only one person fought to save his life  the father Bart tried to kill for  was my last surviving member of my  natural family and nobody in my family  wants to see him executed in February  2018 the execution date is set Kent now  remarried made one last desperate plea  for mercy to the Texas Board of Pardons  and Paroles we're not asking them to  forgive him or to let him go we just  want them to let him live just two days  before the death sentence was to be  carried out the board made its  recommendation the recommendation for  clemency  this was the right thing to do but the  final decision had to come from the  governor and when execution day arrived  there was still no word from him Bart  Whitaker had his last meal Kent went to  see his son for the last time  touched hands through the glass and they  said our goodbyes the clock was ticking  one hour to go would it be life or death  then Peter Van Sant is with us Peter how  unusual is it to have a death sentence  commuted in Texas it's very unusual in  fact Texas executes five times as many  people as the next closest state and at  the time that this appeal was made no  clemency had been granted for more than  a decade  Bart's show any remorse for what he had  done well I was in the courtroom during  actual trial and that was and he took  the stand he said he was sorry for what  he did but as i sat there I felt as  though it was just words his father said  during the course of his time in prison  that he did change and he believes he is  remorseful  so we'll see he's not a very sympathetic  fellow he hired someone to murder his  entire family to have his father as its  number-one advocate who's right standing  it was Brad never seen anything like it  Peter thank you thank you and you can  watch his full report on 48 hours  tomorrow at 10:00 9:00 central right  here on CBS
 

